  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1205 AM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nThe heat continues to be a concern, but heat indices are expected to \nbe generally less than 110 today. This is due to drier conditions \nwith slightly lower dewpoints/RH values. A few locations may exceed \n110 briefly. We may reach Heat Advisory criteria Wednesday, but it \nwill be borderline. Heat Advisory conditions are less likely through \nthe remainder of the week and into the weekend due to increasing \nrain chances/cooler temperatures. This will lead to the major to \nextreme risk of heat related impacts today to lower to a moderate to \nmajor risk the rest of the week.\n\nSlightly drier conditions in the mid levels and PWATs generally less \nthan 2 inches today will keep rain chances low to very low across \nmost of S TX. Although a rogue shower along the sea breeze can not \nbe ruled out. \n\nModels have consistently shown a surge of deeper moisture advecting \nacross S TX beginning Wednesday with increasing rain chances through \nThursday. PWATs are progged to increase to as much as 2.4 inches, \nwhich will be conducive for locally heavy rain creating localized \nflooding problems, especially if the storms are slow moving. Highest \nrain chances Thursday are expected across the eastern half of S TX \nwith PoPs from 35-60% and <35% closer to the Rio Grande. \n\nRain chances begin to drop off Friday to a 20-35% chance then 5-20% \nover the weekend as mid level ridging takes hold. Sufficient \nmoisture should remain in place early next week for a 10-20% chance \nof showers and storms along the daily sea breeze. \n\nModels show a long easterly fetch developing across the northern \ngulf waters this week which may help push water farther up the \nbeaches. Will continue to monitor trends for slightly higher than \nnormal tides and/or an increased rip current risk.  At this time, \nminor coastal flooding is not anticipated but will continue to be \nmonitored.",
